Architectural Diagnosis: Top 3 Bottlenecks & Code Smells
1. The SwiftUI–AppKit Responder Loop on the Hot Keystroke Path
Binding TextKit2TypingEditor directly to a @Published property (typedText / editorText) inside a monolithic ObservableObject forces every single keystroke through a high-overhead cycle:
 * NSTextView processes keyDown and mutates NSTextContentStorage.
 * Delegate fires, writing back to the @Published binding on TypingScreenViewModel.
 * objectWillChange.send() broadcasts to the entire TypingScreenView hierarchy (ReferenceCodeView, PracticeInputPanel, status chips, progress bars).
 * SwiftUI diffs the view tree and calls updateNSView(_:context:) back on TextKit2TypingEditor.
 * If updateNSView does not strictly guard against re-entrancy or active Input Method Editor (IME) marked text (hasMarkedText()), it resets NSTextContentStorage attributes or moves the cursor mid-composition—causing frame drops, dead-key bugs, and unnecessary layout invalidations.
2. The "God Object" ViewModel & Implicit State Permutations
TypingScreenViewModel (~330 lines, 20+ @Published properties) conflates four orthogonal responsibilities:
 * Text Buffer & Cursor State: editorText, revealedLength, practicePosition
 * Domain Mode Logic: .copy, .keyLines, .recall, bugHunt: BugHunt?, contrast: ContrastPair?
 * Session Persistence & Telemetry: Core Data saves, TypingStatistics, selfRating
 * User Preferences: textViewFontSize, sessionTabSpaces
Because drill modes are represented as optional sibling properties (bugHunt?, contrast?) alongside an enum mode, the state space contains impossible states (e.g., mode == .recall while bugHunt != nil). Every @Published mutation invalidates every observer, even if only the current character index changed.
3. Naive Grapheme-to-UTF16 Diffing & Rigid Whitespace Coupling
LineDiff.swift iterates via String.Iterator (Extended Grapheme Clusters), normalizes Unicode, and converts indices to UTF-16 NSRange offsets on every keystroke.
 * Algorithmic overhead: Repeatedly calling unicodeScalars normalization and NSRange(_:in:) inside the keystroke loop allocates intermediate strings and traverses from startIndex (O(N) per call).
 * Rigid syntactic matching: Comparing characters linearly treats structural trivia (extra spaces around operators like int a=0; vs int a = 0;) identically to semantic errors. Furthermore, skipping leading whitespace works for single-line indentation, but breaks down on inline comments, trailing whitespace, or language-specific token boundaries.
Domain-by-Domain Engineering Review
1. Swift Concurrency & Modern Swift Evolution
Swift 6 Strict Concurrency (-strict-concurrency=complete)
 * @MainActor Isolation: TypingScreenViewModel, TextKit2TypingView, and NSApplicationDelegate are inherently UI-bound. Annotate TypingScreenViewModel and TextKit2TypingEditor.Coordinator explicitly with @MainActor.
 * Value-Type Domain Models: Ensure PracticeLesson, PracticeLine, LineDiff, BugHunt, ContrastPair, and RecognitionDrill conform explicitly to Sendable. Because they are immutable structs containing value types, this is zero-cost and allows background parsing/validation of lessons without actor hopping.
 * Core Data Sendability: NSManagedObject is not Sendable. Never pass NSManagedObject instances across actor boundaries into SwiftUI views or view models. Instead, map Core Data entities into Sendable domain value types (HistoryRecordDTO, CustomLessonDTO) inside viewContext.perform or newBackgroundContext().perform.
Swift @Observable vs. ObservableObject (Target OS Tradeoff)
Architectural Constraint: Your target OS is macOS 12.0+, whereas the Swift Observation framework (@Observable) requires macOS 14.0 (Sonoma) at runtime because it links against the OS runtime's observation metadata.
 * Option A (Recommended for OSS Developer Tooling): Bump deployment target to macOS 14.0+. Over 92% of active macOS developers run macOS 14 or 15+. Adopting @Observable eliminates objectWillChange broadcast storms—SwiftUI tracks field-level access per view body, meaning typing a character only invalidates the cursor/status view, never ReferenceCodeView unless lineIndex advances.
 * Option B (Keep macOS 12.0+): Split TypingScreenViewModel into granular, narrow ObservableObject sub-controllers (EditorBufferState, LessonSessionController, EditorSettingsStore) so keystroke mutations in EditorBufferState only re-render PracticeInputPanel.
Core Data vs. SwiftData
Verdict: Retain Core Data, wrapped in a Sendable Repository Actor.
SwiftData requires macOS 14+, lacks mature schema migration debugging compared to Core Data, and couples persistence models directly to SwiftUI view lifecycles via @Query. Your current PersistenceController already supports clean in-memory testing (/dev/null store description). Wrap NSPersistentContainer in a dedicated PersistenceRepository actor that exposes async/await methods returning plain Sendable structs.
2. TextKit 2 Engine & Input Pipeline Optimization
IME & International Input Handling
When a user types an accented character (e.g., Option + e, then e -> é) or uses a CJK IME, AppKit enters a marked text composition session via NSTextInputClient.
 * During composition, textView.hasMarkedText() is true.
 * Rule: Never run LineDiff, auto-advance lines, or overwrite NSTextContentStorage attributes inside textDidChange(_:) while textView.hasMarkedText() is true. Wait until unmarkText() completes and hasMarkedText() returns false.
Achieving a Sub-8ms Keystroke Latency Budget (120Hz ProMotion)
To stay well under the 8.33ms frame budget on ProMotion displays:
 * Bypass SwiftUI on the Keystroke Path: Do not bind typedText: Binding<String> back to SwiftUI on every keystroke. Keep the source of truth for the currently active line buffer inside TextKit2TypingView / Coordinator.
 * Apply Error Highlighting via TextKit 2 Rendering Attributes: In TextKit 2, modifying NSTextContentStorage attributes directly invalidates the entire paragraph layout and forces glyph regeneration. Instead, use NSTextLayoutManager.setRenderingAttributes(_:for:) (macOS 12+). Rendering attributes are ephemeral visual overrides (foreground color, background red tint, underline) applied during drawing without mutating NSTextStorage or re-running layout!
 * Emit High-Level Domain Events Only: The AppKit view only notifies the ViewModel via closures/callbacks on semantic transitions: .progressUpdated(matchedCount: Int, errorRange: NSRange?), .lineCompleted, or .escapePressed.
Consolidating the 4 Editor Views
Retire ModernTypingEditor.swift and TypingEditor.swift immediately. Keep a strict 2-file boundary in Views/Editor/:
 * TextKit2TypingView.swift: Pure AppKit NSView subclass owning the TextKit 2 stack (NSTextContentStorage, NSTextLayoutManager, NSTextContainer, NSTextView). Zero SwiftUI imports—making it directly unit-testable in XCTest / Swift Testing without hosting a SwiftUI view hierarchy.
 * TextKit2TypingEditor.swift: Lightweight NSViewRepresentable adapter (~60 lines) bridging configuration and semantic callbacks to SwiftUI.
3. Syntax-Aware Diffing & Tokenization
Lightweight Native Lexer vs. Tree-sitter
Bundling Tree-sitter and 13 language C-grammars adds ~15–25MB to the binary and complicates universal (arm64 + x86_64) build scripts. You don't need a full AST to fix whitespace/formatting friction—you need a Zero-Allocation Trivia-Normalizing Scanner.
Every line of code across your 13 languages consists of four lexical categories:
 * String/Char Literals ("...", '...'): Exact whitespace inside quotes matters ("hello  world" \neq "hello world").
 * Identifiers & Keywords ([a-zA-Z0-9_]): Whitespace separating two identifiers is mandatory (int left \neq intleft).
 * Operators & Punctuation (=, +, ==, ;, (, )): Whitespace between an identifier and punctuation is optional trivia (int left=0; \equiv int left = 0;).
 * Comments (//, #, /*): Skippable based on skipComments.
By scanning UTF-8 bytes or Unicode scalars into lightweight token spans before diffing, you can support two comparison modes:
 * Strict Character Mode: Exact match (current behavior, with pre-normalized NFC strings).
 * Relaxed Token Mode: Collapses non-semantic horizontal whitespace around punctuation/operators while enforcing exact matches inside string literals and between adjacent identifiers.
4. State Architecture & Domain State Machine Decoupling
Replace the 20+ @Published property bag in TypingScreenViewModel with an explicit, hierarchical Sum-Type State Machine.
 * Mutually Exclusive Drill Modes: Model .standardPractice, .bugHunt, .contrast, and .recognition as an enum with associated values. It becomes a compile-time error to access bugHunt state while in .standardPractice.
 * Pure Reducer Function: Extract state transitions (handleKeystroke, advanceLine, submitBugLine, toggleMode) into a pure, deterministic reduce(state: inout SessionState, action: SessionAction) -> Effect method. Unit tests can now verify every mode transition in microseconds without instantiating AppKit views or UserDefaults.
5. Testability, Quality Assurance & Property-Based Testing
Property-Based / Fuzz Testing for LineDiff
Use Swift's native Testing framework (import Testing) combined with deterministic pseudo-random Unicode generators (SplitMix64 RNG seeded in tests) to test invariants across 10,000 generated inputs:
 * Invariant 1 (Range Safety): For any arbitrary expected: String and typed: String containing emojis, ZWJ sequences (👨‍👩‍👧‍👦), combining marks (e\u{301}), and CJK characters, the returned errorUTF16Range must always verify NSMaxRange(range) <= (typed as NSString).length.
 * Invariant 2 (NFC/NFD Equivalence): LineDiff.compare(expected: s.precomposedStringWithCanonicalMapping, typed: s.decomposedStringWithCanonicalMapping) must always yield .exactMatch.
 * Invariant 3 (Prefix Monotonicity): If typed is a valid prefix of expected, firstMismatch must be nil and isComplete must be typed == expected.
Headless Visual Snapshot Testing on macOS
Avoid third-party frameworks if maintaining zero runtime dependencies: write a lightweight 40-line NSView.snapshotImage() helper in TypingPallTests using view.bitmapImageRepForCachingDisplay(in: view.bounds) and view.cacheDisplay(in: view.bounds, to: rep). Pin the color space to NSColorSpace.sRGB and force view.appearance = NSAppearance(named: .darkAqua) so snapshots are deterministic across developer laptops and GitHub Actions headless runners.
6. Developer Experience, CI/CD & Build Tooling
Optimizing scripts/check.sh & check_lessons.py
 * Content-Addressable Caching in check_lessons.py: Compiling hundreds of C++, Rust, and Go snippets on every commit takes minutes. Compute a SHA-256 hash of (compiler_version, snippet_code, compiler_flags) and store verified hashes in .cache/lesson_hashes.json. Only invoke rustc/g++ on modified or uncached lessons—cutting check_lessons.py runtimes from minutes to < 150ms.
 * Parallel xcodebuild with DerivedData Caching: Ensure check.sh passes -parallel-testing-enabled YES and -maximum-concurrent-test-simulator-destinations (or concurrent macOS test workers) with -resultBundlePath build/TestResults.xcresult.
